WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.000
Time-hopping cops with a budget that only lets&nbsp;
them travel to the '80s?? A cyborg satire watered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.466
down for mass consumption?! The movies are&nbsp;
classics. The TV spin-offs flopped hard.

00:00:11.466 --> 00:00:17.280
A surprise success, "Alien Nation" hit the&nbsp;
big screen in 1988 with a story set three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:23.220
years after an alien race called the Newcomers&nbsp;
arrive on Earth and integrate into human society.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:28.800
James Caan stars as Detective Matt Sykes, who's&nbsp;
partnered with Newcomer Detective Sam Francisco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.840
played by Mandy Patinkin, to hunt down a&nbsp;
killer on the loose. A mix of crime noir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.580
science fiction, and buddy cop tropes, the&nbsp;
film yielded a TV spin-off one year later.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:44.760
This new version, as reimagined by series creator&nbsp;
Kenneth Johnson, is less a buddy cop action series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.980
and more of a gritty cop drama that explores&nbsp;
real issues. In an interview with Games Radar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:55.140
Johnson said he wanted to use the science fiction&nbsp;
elements of the film to examine social and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:00.840
political problems and racial politics. On top of&nbsp;
the police stories, the series put a focus on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:06.000
problems of bigotry faced by Francisco's alien&nbsp;
family as they integrate into Earth culture.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.140
The series garnered mixed reviews upon&nbsp;
release, and ratings at the time weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.420
strong enough to keep it afloat, leading to&nbsp;
cancellation after just 22 episodes. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.160
perhaps due to the success of "The X-Files"&nbsp;
later that decade, Fox was able to resurrect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:25.980
"Alien Nation" as a series of five television&nbsp;
movies, with its final entry landing in 1997.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.529
"See, I haven't gone completely&nbsp;
human." "Yeah dad, it looks good!"

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:35.040
"Logan's Run" established itself as a&nbsp;
groundbreaking sci-fi adventure film when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:40.500
premiered in 1976. The film stars Michael York,&nbsp;
who more modern audiences might know as Basil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.300
Exposition from the cast of the "Austin Powers"&nbsp;
movies. The story is set in a bizarre future world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:51.600
where the government controls its resources by&nbsp;
executing citizens when they turn 30. Logan 5, aka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.400
York, is a former government enforcer who begins&nbsp;
to challenge the system when he meets a young&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.800
woman named Jessica 6. Together the pair go on the&nbsp;
run through post-apocalyptic America in search of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.540
Sanctuary, a legendary utopia where they believe&nbsp;
they can live out the rest of their natural lives.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.980
Just a year after the film was released, a&nbsp;
television adaptation arrived with a new cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:15.720
serving as a retelling and continuation of&nbsp;
the story. The show chronicles much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.200
of the characters' turbulent escape&nbsp;
to the mythical city of Sanctuary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.100
and gives them a robot sidekick&nbsp;
named Rem. Along their travels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:29.460
Logan and Jessica encounter aliens, monsters,&nbsp;
and robots, as well as other runners, rebels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.540
and new cities. They have adventures in&nbsp;
dreams and even do a bit of time traveling.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.720
An ambitious series that may have bitten&nbsp;
off a bit more than it could chew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.400
"Logan's Run" couldn't keep up with other&nbsp;
science fiction tales of the era. It lasted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.837
less than a year and completed just&nbsp;
14 episodes before being canceled.

00:02:45.837 --> 00:02:50.400
While HBO's "Westworld" reboot series is&nbsp;
the franchise's most popular entry today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.740
it's originally based on a 1973 science&nbsp;
fiction film of the same name written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:59.340
by "Jurassic Park" author Michael&nbsp;
Crichton. The movie even received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:04.800
a sequel in 1976 called "Futureworld," but&nbsp;
what's rarely talked about is the spin-off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.000
television series broadcast later&nbsp;
that same year, "Beyond Westworld."

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.100
Where the films see malfunctions lead to disaster&nbsp;
in the titular android theme park, the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:18.960
took a new angle. "Beyond Westworld" features&nbsp;
a recurring villain, the sinister Simon Quaid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:24.480
who's stolen androids from the park for his own&nbsp;
nefarious purposes. Security chief John Moore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.620
played by Jim McMullan, sets out to&nbsp;
capture a new android replicant each week.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.620
A promising premise, it may have&nbsp;
been a little ahead of its time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.100
and it never quite took off. The&nbsp;
fact that only five episodes were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:39.177
ever produced may account for why&nbsp;
the series has faded into obscurity.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.720
Based on the Philip K. Dick short&nbsp;
story "We Can Remember It for You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.000
Wholesale," the big budget 1990 sci-fi film&nbsp;
"Total Recall" is an over-the-top action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.320
adventure led by Arnold Schwarzenegger.&nbsp;
Schwarzenegger stars as Douglas Quaid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.700
a construction worker who opts to have&nbsp;
memory implants of an exotic secret agent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:02.100
life as part of a virtual vacation package.&nbsp;
But the procedure goes wrong, and Quaid soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:06.600
discovers that he may be an undercover agent&nbsp;
for real, sending him on a mission to Mars.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.200
"See you at the party, Richter."

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.380
A TV spin-off titled "Total Recall&nbsp;
2070" launched on Showtime in 1997.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:22.320
Despite its title, "Total Recall 2070" acts more&nbsp;
like a spin-off of a different iconic sci-fi film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:28.380
the 1982 classic "Blade Runner." In the series,&nbsp;
we meet Michael Easton's Detective David Hume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:33.000
of the Citizens Protection Bureau, who's&nbsp;
reluctantly paired with a synthetic partner in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.980
the straight-laced replicant Ian Farve, played&nbsp;
by Karl Pruner. The mismatched cops are tasked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:42.960
with investigating all manner of crimes in the&nbsp;
city, many of which involve the Consortium — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.700
group of powerful mega corporations that&nbsp;
includes virtual vacation agency Rekall.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:53.040
Another ambitious series, "Total Recall&nbsp;
2070" works pretty well at times as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.520
cyberpunk detective series. But with&nbsp;
a high budget and low viewership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.980
the network dropped it after a single season.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:03.660
"Consider that a divorce."

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:08.940
In 1968, Charlton Heston's "Planet&nbsp;
of the Apes" became a huge hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:13.320
telling the upside-down story of an astronaut&nbsp;
who lands on a world where apes rule and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:17.820
humans are slaves. With its shocking twist&nbsp;
ending, it became a cultural phenomenon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:23.220
and the studio rushed a sequel into&nbsp;
production. Between 1970 and 1973,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.420
there were four more entries in the series.&nbsp;
But with box office results dwindling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.680
the franchise tried a different approach with&nbsp;
a prime-time network television spin-off.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:37.380
Debuting in 1974, the "Planet of the Apes"&nbsp;
television series takes place some decade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:42.300
after the events of the films, when another&nbsp;
team of 20th-century astronauts crash-land on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:47.220
the world ruled by apes. It sees the return&nbsp;
of the film's star Roddy McDowall, but in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:52.680
new role — that of Galen, a young and curious&nbsp;
chimpanzee who begins to question the origin of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:58.080
his people and is sentenced to death for heresy.&nbsp;
After Galen is rescued by two of the astronauts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:02.880
the trio escape the city and criss-cross the&nbsp;
landscape having weekly adventures fighting ape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:07.740
oppressors. Despite the popularity of the films,&nbsp;
the TV version of "Planet of the Apes" couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:13.200
find much success, and it was canned after just&nbsp;
four months and 14 episodes. The network would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:18.134
try again a year later with an animated&nbsp;
adaptation, which didn't fare much better.

00:06:18.134 --> 00:06:20.768
"What a thick-brained lout that General Urko is."

00:06:20.768 --> 00:06:26.220
Based on the short story by Philip K. Dick, the&nbsp;
2002 action blockbuster "Minority Report" from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:32.460
director Steven Spielberg stars Tom Cruise, Colin&nbsp;
Farrell, and Samantha Morton. In the film's future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:37.680
world, violent crime is eliminated through the&nbsp;
use of telepathic people called Precogs who can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.840
see into the future. But when Precrime officer&nbsp;
John Anderton discovers he's been identified as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:48.120
a future killer, he goes on the run and helps&nbsp;
upend the entire system, freeing the Precogs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:53.040
from forced servitude. A television spin-off&nbsp;
arrived more than a decade later in 2015.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:57.660
Set after the game-changing events of the&nbsp;
film, the series stars Stark Sands as Dash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:01.800
one of the Precogs who's been released, but&nbsp;
who remains haunted by visions of future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:06.780
murders. With a new status quo, Dash teams&nbsp;
up with a Metropolitan Police Detective and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:10.320
tries to help her prevent murders with&nbsp;
his unique abilities. At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.400
dark forces seek to track Dash down&nbsp;
and use his powers for their own ends.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.180
Where the film excels with a unique&nbsp;
futurist vision, exhilarating action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:22.980
and a captivating mystery, the spin-off&nbsp;
TV series is lackluster and pedestrian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:27.600
An ordinary sci-fi procedural, it failed&nbsp;
to live up to the film and got low ratings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:33.240
leading to Fox cutting the show short at just&nbsp;
10 episodes before closing its doors for good.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:39.000
The 1994 Jean-Claude Van Damme’s movie "Timecop"&nbsp;
is a sci-fi adventure that follows Max Walker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:43.980
an agent of the Time Enforcement Commission,&nbsp;
which tracks time-traveling criminals. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:47.580
Walker goes back in time to stop a&nbsp;
killer, he uncovers evidence that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:52.020
powerful political figure has been using the&nbsp;
government's temporal technology to reshape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:56.940
the future into his own dark design. Based&nbsp;
on a comic book of the same name, "Timecop"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:02.580
was a surprise hit in 1994, and a few years&nbsp;
later a television spin-off came to fruition.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:08.220
With a premise tailor-made for weekly adventures,&nbsp;
the small screen adaptation follows the exploits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:13.260
of officer Jack Logan, played by Ted King,&nbsp;
who goes back in time each episode to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:17.520
a criminal in the past. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
the series was dragged down by a lack of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:21.868
ambition and a lower budget that limited&nbsp;
the scope to relatively modern settings.

00:08:21.868 --> 00:08:25.638
"Well, the&nbsp;senate thinks if we spent more time in&nbsp;
the present tracking down rogue sleds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.638 --> 00:08:28.578
fewer criminals would get back.&nbsp;
We'd cut our budget by a third."

00:08:28.668 --> 00:08:32.400
Beyond its inability to dream big, the&nbsp;
"Timecop" show was simply cheap looking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:37.440
poorly produced, and often just plain&nbsp;
boring. In the end, just nine episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:42.000
were aired. The fact that nobody really&nbsp;
remembers this one is sadly no surprise.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:47.340
In 1984, director John Carpenter helmed&nbsp;
a thoughtful and romantic science fiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:52.380
drama called "Starman." The film stars Jeff&nbsp;
Bridges as an alien entity who's stranded on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:56.280
Earth and befriends a young woman named&nbsp;
Jenny, played by Karen Allen. The alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:00.240
is also wearing a body that resembles her&nbsp;
dead husband. While the two fall in love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:04.380
Starman searches for a way to return&nbsp;
to his home. Bridges received an Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:08.040
nomination for Best Actor, and a TV&nbsp;
spin-off arrived two years later.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:13.440
Set more than a decade after the film, "Starman"&nbsp;
the series sees the alien being return to find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:18.360
child he fathered with Jenny. Taking the shape&nbsp;
of photojournalist Paul Forrester, played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:23.460
Robert Hays, he reconnects with his now-teenaged&nbsp;
son, and each week the alien and his human child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:28.320
set out to find his missing mother. At the same&nbsp;
time, they must also outrun the government forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:32.880
who are hunting them, including a determined&nbsp;
investigator who believes them to be a danger.

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:38.100
Focusing on the father and son relationship,&nbsp;
"Starman" the series loses the romance of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:42.360
film, exchanging it for a kid-friendly&nbsp;
adventure. Ultimately, it wasn't enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:46.397
lasting just one season and&nbsp;
being all but forgotten today.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:48.300
"We're gone. We're history."

00:09:48.755 --> 00:09:54.600
Paul Verhoeven's 1987 hit "RoboCop" is a&nbsp;
sci-fi classic with a dystopian view of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.600 --> 00:10:00.000
the future that feels eerily prescient today.&nbsp;
Packed with social allegory that touches on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.260
corporate greed, class inequality, and&nbsp;
the militarization of law enforcement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:09.780
"RoboCop" is much more than a thrilling sci-fi&nbsp;
action adventure. The film stars Peter Weller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:15.300
as Alex Murphy — a cop who's critically wounded&nbsp;
in the line of duty and turned into a cyborg as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:20.097
part of a new police program. "RoboCop" was&nbsp;
one of the best action movies of the decade.

00:10:20.097 --> 00:10:22.810
"Your move, creep."

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:26.640
A pair of big-screen sequels&nbsp;
followed in 1990 and 1993,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:29.580
and a live-action TV spin-off landed a year later.

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:34.560
With a fairly sizable budget for its day, the&nbsp;
series looked pretty good at the time. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:38.460
the creators returned "RoboCop" to its&nbsp;
roots with plenty of social commentary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:43.620
being confined to television meant ditching the&nbsp;
ultra-violence of the films, which turned off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:47.880
some fans. However, the addition of Murphy's&nbsp;
parents expanded the character's story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:51.900
and the return of the classic satirical&nbsp;
commercials was a highlight as well.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:57.034
"Be a superhero. Be a super shopper!"

00:10:57.034 --> 00:11:01.427
Disappointing ratings saw the network fail&nbsp;
to renew the series for a second season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.500
though producers tried another live-action reboot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:09.165
a few years later in 2001 with the&nbsp;
TV miniseries "Prime Directives."

00:11:09.165 --> 00:11:13.320
The sci-fi comedy "Weird Science" was&nbsp;
written and directed by John Hughes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:17.880
whose string of teen comedies made him&nbsp;
an '80s legend. The film starred Anthony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:22.680
Michael Hall and Ian Michael Smith as Gary&nbsp;
and Wyatt, a pair of high school geeks who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:27.420
use their computer to engineer the perfect&nbsp;
woman, played by model Kelly LeBrock. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:31.860
creating their dream girl — the much older&nbsp;
Lisa — the two nerdy teens get into a series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:36.840
of comical misadventures and wacky hijinks.&nbsp;
Lisa uses her own magical powers to help them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:42.118
deal with their problems in life and love.&nbsp;
Bill Paxton and Robert Downey Jr. also star.

00:11:42.118 --> 00:11:44.700
"Now, I want you to promise that you'll keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:48.480
your big mouth shut about everything&nbsp;
that has happened here this weekend."

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:53.460
A decade after the movie, a TV series arrived,&nbsp;
retelling the events of the film and giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:58.620
viewers weekly adventures of Gary, Wyatt,&nbsp;
and Lisa. Playing up Lisa's magical powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:03.360
the series made the character into more of a&nbsp;
genie who could grant wishes, rather than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:07.800
protective helper to the two boys. But thanks&nbsp;
to its status as a cable network original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:12.720
the series could venture into more risque&nbsp;
territory just like the film. Never a major hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:17.400
the series received mixed reviews, but&nbsp;
this was no single-season flop. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:21.501
the show ran for five seasons&nbsp;
between the USA Network and Syfy.

00:12:21.501 --> 00:12:26.856
Despite its 88-episode run, however, "Weird&nbsp;
Science" isn't talked about much these days.

